      "response_en": "Anxiety is like your brain's alarm system, designed to keep you safe. When it senses a threat, it releases adrenaline and cortisol into your body. This causes your heart to race, your breathing to change, and your muscles to tighten. It's meant to help you react quickly to danger, like running from a lion. However, your brain sometimes misfires this alarm on non-physical threats, like a difficult meeting or a strong emotion. This is why you might feel those physical symptoms even when you're not in actual danger.\n\nUnderstanding the anxiety cycle can help manage it. It starts with a trigger, followed by a thought that your brain interprets as a threat. This leads to a physical response, and then a behavior, like avoiding the situation. The physical sensations are real, but they are your body's way of trying to protect you.\n\nDoes any part of this explanation resonate with what you've been experiencing?"
